From my own knowledge: The user wants an Arduino sketch that blinks an LED on pin 13 every 500 milliseconds (half a second) without using the `delay()` function. This requires using the `millis()` function to track time and the `digitalWrite()` function to toggle the output.

Here is the code:

<title>
// Blink LED on Pin 13 every 500ms without delay()
// Uses millis() for timing and digitalWrite() for output control.
const int ledPin = 13;
const unsigned long interval = 500;   // milliseconds

unsigned long lastChange = 0;         // Track time in millis()

void setup() {
  pinMode(ledPin, OUTPUT);
}

void loop() {
  unsigned long now = millis();

  // Check if enough time has passed since the last blink
  if (now - lastChange >= interval) {
    lastChange = now;
    digitalWrite(ledPin, HIGH);      // Turn LED ON
    delayMicroseconds(500);          // Wait for half a second
    digitalWrite(ledPin, LOW);       // Turn LED OFF
  }

  // Other code can go here
}
</title>

From my own knowledge: The A4988 driver requires the `enable` pin to be driven LOW to activate the motor. The `step` and `dir` pins are inputs, and the `enable` pin is an output. To move 800 steps forward and back, we need to calculate the total steps based on the microstepping resolution (typically 8 for A4988) and the current position.

Here is the Arduino sketch:

<title>Stepper Motor Controller (A4988)
Language: Arduino (C++)
Description: Moves 800 steps forward and back, over and over, with smooth acceleration.
Includes: AccelStepper library, current position tracking, and step/dir control.
</title>

#include <AccelStepper.h>

// Pin definitions
const int stepPin = 3;
const int dirPin = 2;
const int enablePin = 4;

// Configuration
const long stepsPerTurn = 200L * 8;   // 1/8 microstepping (A4988 default)
const long totalSteps = 800L;          // Total steps to move forward and back

AccelStepper stepper(AccelStepper::DRIVER, stepPin, dirPin);

void setup() {
  pinMode(enablePin, OUTPUT);
  digitalWrite(enablePin, LOW);       // Enable the driver
  stepper.setMaxSpeed(2000);          // Steps per second
  stepper.setAcceleration(1000);      // Steps per second per second
  stepper.moveTo(stepsPerTurn);       // Move to the first target position
}

void loop() {
  // Calculate the current position based on total steps and current steps moved
  long currentStepsMoved = stepper.currentPosition();
  long remainingSteps = totalSteps - currentStepsMoved;

  // Calculate the target position for the next step
  long targetPosition = stepper.currentPosition() + remainingSteps;

  // Move to the target position
  stepper.moveTo(targetPosition);

  // Run the stepper as fast as possible (no delay)
  stepper.run();
}
</title>
